{
  "name": "regex-escaped-pairs-lone-halves",
  "description": "With `u`, Dart reads two 4-digit escapes, a high half then a low half, as one code point, inside a class as outside one: `\\uD801\\uDC00` is the Deseret capital long I \ud801\udc00 (U+10400). So a class range with such an escaped pair at an end compares code points. Swift's SDK read a class's escapes one half at a time, so such a range came out of order and was refused, and it wrote two lone halves side by side, where ICU joined them into the letter; these rows pin Dart's reading on every client. The range `[\\uD801\\uDC00-\\uD801\\uDC27]` (\ud801\udc00 to \ud801\udc27, U+10427) with `giu` on the small \ud801\udc28 (U+10428) gives \"#\" and on a\ud801\udc00b \"a#b\", while with `gu` it leaves \ud801\udc28 unchanged; the negated `[^\\uD801\\uDC00-\\uD801\\uDC27]` with `giu` on \ud801\udc00x gives \"\ud801\udc00#\" and with `gu` on \ud801\udc28 \"#\". An end braced or literal reads the same: `[\\uD801\\uDC00-\\u{10427}]` with `giu` on \ud801\udc28 and `[\\u{10400}-\\uD801\\uDC27]` with `gu` on \ud801\udc27 give \"#\", `[\ud801\udc00-\\uD801\\uDC27]` with `giu` on \ud801\udc28 gives \"#\" and `[\\uD801\\uDC00-\ud801\udc27]` with `gu` on \ud801\udc00x \"#x\". Among other members: `[a\\uD801\\uDC00-\\uD801\\uDC27b]` with `giu` on a\ud801\udc00b gives \"###\" and `[\\w\\uD801\\uDC00-\\uD801\\uDC27]` with `gu` on x\ud801\udc00 \"##\". The pair-to-same-pair range `[\\uD801\\uDC00-\\uD801\\uDC00]` with `giu` on \ud801\udc28 gives \"#\" and with `gu` on \ud801\udc00\ud801\udc27 \"#\ud801\udc27\"; the emoji range `[\\uD83D\\uDE00-\\uD83D\\uDE4F]` with `giu` on \ud83d\ude00 gives \"#\"; and `[\\uD801\\uDC00-\\uD801\\uDC27]+` with `giu` on \ud801\udc00\ud801\udc27 gives \"#\" and with `gu` on x\ud801\udc00 \"x#\". Two halves spelled by escapes Dart does not read as a pair, one or both braced, are two lone halves, which match nothing in a string of whole characters: `\\uD801\\u{DC00}` with `giu` on \ud801\udc00 and with `gu` on \ud801\udc00x, `\\u{D801}\\uDC00` with `gu` on \ud801\udc00, `\\u{D801}\\u{DC00}` with `giu` on \ud801\udc00, `\\uD801\\u{0DC00}` with `gu` on \ud801\udc00, `\\uD801\\u{DC00}+` with `giu` on \ud801\udc00\ud801\udc27, `(?i:\\uD801\\u{DC00})` with `gu` on \ud801\udc28, `x\\uD801\\u{DC00}` with `giu` on x\ud801\udc00, \ud83d\ude00's halves `\\uD83D\\u{DE00}` with `gu` on \ud83d\ude00, and the class `[\\uD801\\u{DC00}]` with `giu` on \ud801\udc00 and with `gu` on a\ud801\udc00b each leave the input unchanged. The pair as two 4-digit escapes, `\\uD801\\uDC00` with `giu` on \ud801\udc00, still gives \"#\". Every regex puts \"#\" for each match. Every input reads page state, so the engine keeps the Values unresolved with `watches` and the client resolves them.",
